WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.311 --> 00:00:01.350
(train engine revving)

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.760
- Living in New York can feel like

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.180
you're constantly bombarded
with stressful sounds 24/7.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.150
(cars hooting)
(alarm sounding)

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.759
So can sounds like these

00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:13.800
(instruments tinging)

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.860
help with stress management and anxiety?

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.200
To get answers, I went to a university lab

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.360
to get hooked up to a
brain recording device.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.230
Feels good.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.930
And tracked my heart rate
at a sound bath in Brooklyn.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.470
During sound baths or sound meditations

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.989
a facilitator like Mattan
Sharvit plays instruments.

00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:34.448
(instruments humming)

00:00:34.448 --> 00:00:37.629
(bright gentle music)

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:39.248
(drums banging)

00:00:39.248 --> 00:00:41.250
(chimes chiming)

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.980
- So the idea is to really
listen to the instruments,

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.413
trust yourself and let go.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.510
- [Narrator] For the nearly
two hour group session

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.270
Mattan told us to move
as little as possible.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.085
That was actually easier
than I anticipated.

00:00:57.085 --> 00:01:00.252
(instruments humming)

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.580
- There is never same feeling.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.020
There is never the same trip.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.550
It's always different.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.160
- It kind of felt ominous and scary,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.230
but then also like really grounding

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.467
and that was unexpected, but nice.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:18.840
(gentle organ music)

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.160
To understand the science

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.710
behind how music affects
the brain and body,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.173
I drove up to Yale
University in New Haven.

00:01:26.576 --> 00:01:30.900
There I met AZA Allsop, a
musician and neuroscientist

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.600
who studies the effects
of music on the brain.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.570
- Some of what sound baths
are actually tapping into

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:38.700
as well is this idea of meditation.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.460
It's sort of getting
your mind into a place

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.590
that is more still.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.550
- AZA and Mattan told me

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.363
that music helps us get into a
meditative state more easily.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.270
What about music facilitates that?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.340
- Some of it we think is
the actual physiology.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.340
So the fact that your heart
rate is now slowing down,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.900
your breathing is slowing down

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.810
when you have these
very long sort of tones.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.400
- [Narrator] During my sound meditation

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.160
Mattan played tones that
lingered for a long time

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.903
and that were bright and light.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.260
This was among like,
my lowest heart rates.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:14.970
- So right now I hear a lot
of longer, higher frequencies.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.250
- [Narrator] Some studies have
found that sounds like these

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.860
stimulate the parasympathetic
nervous system.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.180
It's a network of nerves

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.570
that helps run involuntary
bodily functions like digestion.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.650
When the activity of the

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.320
parasympathetic nervous system increases

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.420
blood pressure and heart rate go down

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.283
and you feel more relaxed.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.160
During my visit, I tried out a session

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.950
of AZA's online music mindfulness study.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.030
In his participants,

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.210
he's seeing lower levels
of perceived stress.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.070
I was surprised that I also felt

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.870
calmer and more relaxed here,

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:47.010
even though the setting

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.354
wasn't as soothing as the Yoga studio.

00:02:49.354 --> 00:02:52.650
(drum sounding)

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.720
But music can also be intense

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.853
and bring up unsettling emotions.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.270
- The harmonics are a
little bit more dissonant.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.270
There's a little bit more tension there.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.270
- [Narrator] In some cases,
sounds with these qualities

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.340
can even activate the amygdala,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.680
which is involved in our fear response.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.150
- When we feel that emotion

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.483
we might cry or have some
sort of cathartic experience.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:18.210
- AZA's brain imaging research

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.550
is trying to decode what kinds of music

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.000
can improve our mental health
and under what circumstances.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.590
One of his initial findings

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.830
is that certain kinds of music
facilitates social connection

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.600
which can also relieve stress

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.180
and have positive health benefits.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.350
AZA and his team hooked me up

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.750
to dozens of brain activity sensors

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.030
to test music's effects on brain activity

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.060
and how connected I
felt to another person.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.050
I'm all hooked up.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.390
In this case, head of
the brain function lab,

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.389
neuroscientist Joy Hirsch.

00:03:47.389 --> 00:03:48.968
Hello.

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:51.090
We felt most connected

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.710
when we were listening to
harmonious music like this

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.953
and looking at each other.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.700
That's in part because the
temporal parietal junction,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.670
an area that helps with
social information processing

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.693
is more active.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.637
And it's less active when
the music is dissonant.

00:04:07.637 --> 00:04:10.220
(gentle music)

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.130
Feeling connected is important

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.250
because isolation is a
growing mental health problem.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.490
58% of Americans say they're lonely.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:24.060
That figure is even higher
among Hispanic and Black adults

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.100
for whom AZA and other experts say

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.190
there are big unmet mental health needs.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:31.950
One barrier to accessing
sound meditation sessions

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.740
and their potential benefits is cost.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.360
The other is time.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.580
But research shows that even a few minutes

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.129
of pairing music and mindfulness can help.

00:04:41.129 --> 00:04:43.712
(gentle music)

